  "summary": "A variable frequency drive (VFD, inverter) controls the speed of an AC motor by changing the frequency and voltage it supplies. The rectifier turns mains AC into DC, the DC link capacitors smooth it, and the IGBT inverter switches the DC rapidly (PWM) to build a new AC waveform of any frequency. Running pumps and fans at reduced speed instead of throttling saves a lot of energy (affinity laws: power ∝ speed³).",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Power electronic motor drive",
    "type": "Voltage-source inverter, 6-pulse diode front end, IGBT PWM"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "animation": "Motor speed ramps up, runs, and ramps down as the output frequency changes",
    "overlay": "Waveforms through the drive"
  },
